WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.669
Film Courage: Can you share 5 lessons that
will help someone be a better filmmaker right

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:04.669
now?

00:00:04.669 --> 00:00:07.959
Matt Eskandari, Writer/Director/Producer:
Five lessons that will make you a better filmmaker

00:00:07.959 --> 00:00:13.200
right now is first trust your actors.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.540
Actors are always going to come in there to
try to elevate the material, to try and create

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.369
better work for you.

00:00:20.369 --> 00:00:22.830
So always trust your actors.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:28.939
Number 2 - Don’t get so trapped on what
your vision is that you’re so focused on

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:36.710
it you can’t listen to otters ideas or people
because that will limit what you can get.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:43.610
Third is don’t give up you know what I mean
if things aren’t exactly going your way

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:48.220
that doesn’t mean that there isn’t something
right around the corner or something you can

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.890
do to kind of get to that quicker.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:55.470
I think another tip is to read a lot of scripts.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:01:00.890
I think reading scripts and then watching
the film actually made off of the scripts

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:04.960
you will learn so much about how the story
was actually executed.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.130
It’s one thing to see a movie and be like
“Oh this is how I would have done it.”

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.900
Or “This is how they should have done it.”

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.840
But when you read the actual material that
it was based on (the blueprint itself) and

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:15.840
then see “Oh?

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.000
That was interesting the way the director
executed that scene or the blocking in that

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:28.619
scene was different than the way I would have
blocked it but that was interesting.

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:35.800
Another bit of advice that I would give is
edit projects as much as you can because the

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:42.359
more as a director you’re on set thinking
as an editor, the faster you will be, the

00:01:42.359 --> 00:01:48.979
more concise you will be and the more able
you will get what you need on set because

00:01:48.979 --> 00:01:54.170
you’ve spent that time in the edit bay seeing
what works, seeing how cuts and takes work

00:01:54.170 --> 00:02:00.659
and cut get together and elements you kind
of don’t need and things that you do need

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:01.789
really helps with that.

00:02:01.789 --> 00:02:07.150
So I think cutting projects, cutting stuff
on your own is really essential.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:13.480
I remember one of the things that I did in
film school was I would take a scene from

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:19.220
from whatever your favorite movies are whether
it’s LAWRENCE OF ARABIA or THE GODFATHER

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.960
or whatever, you take those films and you
take a scene and drop it on the [editing software]

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:29.230
timeline and you just find all of the cuts
for that scene and you look at it in whatever

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.250
editing program you are using and you’re
like “Oh?

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:34.190
So they cut to that…to that…to that.”

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:40.330
And it really is an invaluable way to see
how a scene plays out.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:43.909
How cuts can get either shorter or faster.

00:02:43.909 --> 00:02:50.000
How the director and the editor creates suspense
or tension by the way that the cuts are laid

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.000
out in the scene.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.269
That was a really invaluable lesson is by
looking at other projects and seeing how they

00:02:56.269 --> 00:02:57.470
were cut.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.550
Film Courage: That’s excellent.

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:05.340
Do you remember the films you used to watch
again [in film school]?

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:12.310
Some of the ones that really stuck out to
you?

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:26.099
Matt: It’s hard for me to choose five films
or just a list of films that kind of left

00:03:26.099 --> 00:03:27.640
an impression on me as a filmmaker.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:34.739
I think more than anything it’s the directors
themselves and the body of work that I look

00:03:34.739 --> 00:03:36.910
back on it and get inspiration from.

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:43.239
Like look at [Stanley] Kubrick and how he
was able to tap into different genres like

00:03:43.239 --> 00:03:49.000
horror, the war films and all of those different
genres that he touched on.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.689
How he would uniquely shoot a scene was interesting.

00:03:52.689 --> 00:03:54.200
This would always give me inspiration.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:59.269
I was like “That was a different way to
shoot that scene?”

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:05.769
So I’m now trying to think of other directors
or other filmmakers and see how at their work

00:04:05.769 --> 00:04:10.659
they might have approached a similar scene
and try to be like “Oh that’s different?

00:04:10.659 --> 00:04:12.530
I never would have thought of that?”

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:20.609
But that all kind of inspired me as a filmmaker
I mean Kubrick, Spielberg, Kurosawa, Christopher

00:04:20.609 --> 00:04:27.620
Nolan those are the type of filmmakers where
their work is so unique and such a strong

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:33.870
voice that anytime I need inspiration and
want to just kind of draw back into that creative

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:38.669
well I can just look at any of their films
and find something unique or different that

00:04:38.669 --> 00:04:40.819
will help motivate and inspire me.

00:04:40.819 --> 00:04:45.450
Film Courage: Speaking of chess (we talked
about it earlier in the interview) Kubrick

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.390
used to play chess for money?

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:56.750
Matt: Kubrick approached the filmmaking process
like a chess game, every problem that came

00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:00.850
at him was another move on the board.

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:07.360
He would always try to anticipate two steps
ahead, other things that could happen.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:14.060
Chess is not just an analogy for filmmaking,
it’s really an analogy for life where if

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:19.870
you can become a good chess player (if you
put yourself in that mindset) you can become

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:26.740
a better filmmaker and hopefully a better
person because you can kind of look at life

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:27.740
from that perspective.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:31.810
Film Courage: He [Kubrick] had a very unique
way of looking at the world…a contrarian

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:35.260
view in a lot of ways (from interview with
him).

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:36.260
Very interesting guy.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:37.260
Matt: Totally.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:37.570
Fascinating figure.

